| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Territorial Diving |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Breeding Defender ''Paired Radius'' is a breeding pair that divides one tower into upper and lower territory. This division does not carry over to the communal roost; if one bird is absent, only clashes with neighboring pairs increase. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0175"></a>

### Role and Body

In the less-cold weeks, the bodies of a breeding pair that split one tower into upper and lower halves to guard it made the radius. The male on the upper level read helmet outlines first and the female on the lower level read the angle of poles first, and dives from two directions went for the edge of the work basket at the same time.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0177"></a>

### Origin

On top of the territorial defense that large-billed crows kept in the breeding season was laid a division of labor in which one tower was split in two for sitting. The repetition of sparks and the appearance of workers taught the length of the radius, and when one side vanished, a neighboring pair tried out the empty place and clashes grew.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0179"></a>

### Habitat

The breeding radius was fixed to the upper and lower arms of the factory-roof towers, and outside the breeding season the division came loose and joined the communal roost. The skies over the bridge and the school passage were only patrolled, never nested, and when a helmet entered the radius, the patrol stopped and returned to the tower.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0181"></a>

### Needs and Economy

Keeping the radius required nest material and insects together with the gaps in sight left when helmets and poles were away. When the basket crossed the radius every day, defense labor ate into food hauling and the chicks lost weight, and when the basket never went up at all, damage to the sheathing was left alone and the short circuits grew.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0183"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

The lower body brooding the eggs changed places only once every two days, and the upper body carried food during that time. Torn feathers were preened only in the shade of the sunshade cloth, and if the work platform below the tower''s waist was used as shade, the angle of the next dive was redrawn with the platform as its reference.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0185"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

Only the pair divided the radius, and the young helpers added only warning calls. This division was not passed on to the communal roost, and when one breeding individual went missing, a neighboring pair tried out the empty perch and territorial clashes grew.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0187"></a>

### Escalating Threat

When a helmet outline brushed the edge of the radius, only the calls changed. When the outline came inside, dives from both directions opened, and if the sunshade cloth hung only at the waist, the upper beak cut the basket''s ropes and parts fell.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0189"></a>

### Combat and Response

While the breeding pair guarded the upper and lower tower at once, a helmet outline entering the radius opened dives from two directions. Hanging sunshade cloth on the upper arm and at the waist at staggered times threw their lines of sight off and scattered the glides onto the railings. If the sunshade cloth was wrapped only around the tower''s waist, the lower beak was blocked but the upper beak tore the edge of the basket.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0191"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Within the breeding radius, the Haeryun Mobile Gate reduced the work baskets to a single line and in return recognized the breeding pair''s upper seat. If the new horn pattern of G02-SC1 was read as food within the radius, that recognition slowed the basket by a day. Pushing the seat aside meant that recognition did not come back until the next breeding season, and the scalps of the helmeted workers paid the price first.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0193"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC1 left the choice, when the horns raised the expectation of food within the radius, between recognizing the upper seat and reducing the baskets. The call misclassification of G02-SC2 read the pair''s warning calls as outage warnings, and in the sea-wind weeks of G02-SC3 the radius briefly dissolved into the communal roost over the bridge.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0195"></a>
<a id="g02e09"></a>

### G02E09 · Radio Crow Sound-Pattern Core ''Mixed Bells''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: second elite
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC3","G02-SC1","G02-SC2"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Common species |
| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Sound-Pattern Amplification |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Sound-Pattern Core ''Mixed Bells'' is a temporary core body that echoes inspection bells learned at different towers above the bridge. It gives no orders, providing only a place where sound patterns converge; when the sea wind shifts, the circle disappears too. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0198"></a>

### Role and Body

When the inspection bells learned by several families gathered into a single point over the Han River bridge, a core body rose that amplified that mixing without a leader. The core matched its wingbeats and rang the bell back, and at a single stroke the flock had already begun to move, so the column behind the umbrellas backed up.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0200"></a>

### Origin

On top of the rule that the nonbreeding communal roost shared only call patterns was laid the occurrence that in weeks when the sea wind and breeding overlapped, the bells of different towers mixed over one bridge. The meaning of the radio waves was not read; only the density of the overlapping ringing remained, and that density made the place of the core.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0202"></a>

### Habitat

The place of the core formed only within a thirty-meter circle above the transmission inspection opening, and when the sea wind turned, the circle vanished with it. When individuals that had learned on the Yeongdeungpo Line roofs and at the Wangsimni Union crossings entered that circle, they exchanged each other''s bells, and on nights when the exchange was over they scattered back to their original towers.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0204"></a>

### Needs and Economy

For the core to hold, the sea wind, breeding, and bells struck without any time lag had to coincide. Striking the bells out of step at each tower made the core fade within three or four days, and refusing the staggering left the tail of the evacuation column on the railings and cut the flow of passage.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0206"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

The core gesture did not last more than a week, and when the sea wind changed, it learned the matching of wings over again from the start. Young individuals that had not finished learning called only at the edge of the circle, and those edge calls widened the gaps in the umbrella lines.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0208"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The core gave no orders and only provided a place where call patterns gathered. The breeding pair''s division of the radius did not work here, and even when H02 singled out one leader, HC02 control divided only the discrepancies in bell-striking times.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0210"></a>

### Escalating Threat

When the bells of different towers stuck together into one tone, the flock began to fill the circle. When strikes without time lag continued, the rear of the umbrellas stopped, and at the third accumulation, evacuees left on the railings stood under the dive still clutching their bags.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0212"></a>

### Combat and Response

When the inspection bells learned at different towers mixed in the sky over a single bridge, the flock had already begun moving at one bell. Staggering the strikes by ten minutes at each tower made the core''s answering ring scatter a beat late. Striking the bells with a time lag at each tower brought the tail of the column in under the umbrellas, and striking them without a lag left the evacuees at the rear on the railings.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0214"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Only when the Yeongdeungpo Line, the Wangsimni Union, and Haeryun entered the communal roost of the sea-wind weeks in one ledger could they divide the striking of the new inspection bells. Entering it in the ledger meant putting up with a few days of relearning but lowered the density of the circle. If the ledger stayed empty, the bridge of G02-SC3 opened, but children and evacuees became the new targets of the dives.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0216"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC3 recorded the week when the sea wind and breeding overlapped and the core rose as the highest stage. When the horn pattern of G02-SC1 mixed into the core''s call pattern, the circle spread as far as the work basket, and the misclassification of G02-SC2 read the core''s answering ring as an outage warning.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0218"></a>
<a id="g02e10"></a>

### G02E10 · Radio Crow Remnant-Tower Organ ''Crossbeam Tone''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: environmental stronghold type
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC2","G02-SC1","G02-SC3"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Habitat site or facility |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Roost Coordinates |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Remnant-Tower Organ ''Crossbeam Tone'' is a place where the crossbeam of the Sangam remnant tower and the wind form one body. With no leader, the birds read its low tone as the number of the arm on which to perch. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0221"></a>

### Role and Body

The moment the crossbar of the Sangam remnant tower gave off a low tone in the wind, the place-type organ in which that crossbar and the wind were one body became the roost''s coordinate. Several families read that tone, without a chief, as the number of the arm they should sit on, and when the tone broke off, droppings and dives came down all at once under the inspection opening.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0223"></a>

### Origin

After the transmission towers left at Sangam and Nanji came to serve as both nests and feeding grounds, the resonance of the crossbars was learned like a safety signal. Not a genetic change but the repeated pairing of wind and insect lamps made the place into an organ, and until a demolition was declared, that learning carried on month after month.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0225"></a>

### Habitat

Outside the breeding season the middle arm of the Sangam remnant tower was the center, and the Nanji side lit up only briefly when the lamps were moved. In the breeding season the function of the place weakened and the pairs'' radii divided up the tower, and in the sea-wind weeks the crossbar tone got crossed with the low tones of the bridge cables.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0227"></a>

### Needs and Economy

For the place to remain an organ, the crossbar had to stay undemolished and there had to be room to move the insect lamps to Nanji. Fixing the lamps to the remnant tower closed the transmission inspection opening with a film of droppings, and merely switching the lamps off moved the families to the waste heat of the factory roofs, eating up the night maintenance window.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0229"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

When the crossbar''s bolts loosened, the tone rose by one note and the coordinate slipped, and tightening them brought it back down. If the lamps were left on when the maintenance crew tightened the bolts at night, the families learned that higher tone as a new coordinate and the next month''s records came out wrong.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0231"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The place was the center, and the families only shared the tone and had no chief. Even when H02 singled out one heat source as the core, the person in charge of control wrote down only the crossbar arm numbers and the lamp positions separately.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0233"></a>

### Escalating Threat

When the low tone repeated for three days, the arm they sat on became fixed. If the lamps could not be pulled out to Nanji, droppings piled on the inspection-opening lid, and dives in the same week closed the lid so that one round of transmission inspection went empty.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0235"></a>

### Combat and Response

When the crossbar of the Sangam remnant tower gave off its low tone in the wind, several families already used that tone as the roost''s coordinate. Moving the lamps to the low arm at Nanji and switching off the Sangam side split the coordinate in a single night. If the insect lamps could not be moved to the Nanji side, the transmission inspection opening under the remnant tower closed in the same week under a film of droppings and dives.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0237"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

The records of the remnant tower''s use outside the breeding season carried over to the next month until one side declared demolition. Recognizing the insect-lamp relocation of G02-SC2 as the Nanji base opened the Sangam inspection opening, but took on that base''s cycle of droppings for another month. Choosing demolition left the factory-roof shift to take on all the night maintenance of that month.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0239"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC2 recorded that the choice of moving the lamps to the disused tower could turn the Sangam crossbar tone into a Nanji base. The work noise of G02-SC1 overlapped with the crossbar''s low tone and fouled the coordinate, and the sea wind of G02-SC3 briefly echoed the same low tone back in the bridge cables.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0241"></a>
<a id="g02e11"></a>

### G02E11 · Radio Crow Bridge Cylinder ''Inspection Hollow''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: environmental stronghold type
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC3","G02-SC1","G02-SC2"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Habitat site or facility |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Pedestrian Blockade |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Bridge Cylinder ''Inspection Hollow'' is a fortress formed when the sea wind fills a cylinder of air above the transmission inspection hatch. Wings within the cylinder divide up altitude without direction, determining who can cross without an umbrella. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0244"></a>

### Role and Body

When the column of air above the transmission inspection opening filled with sea wind, that space became a fortress that split the walkway. The wings inside the circle divided only altitude without a leader, and on a crossing without umbrellas, beaks and flashlights met on one line and bag straps were torn.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0246"></a>

### Origin

As the bridge cables and the sky above the inspection opening became a communal roost for several families, the fortress arose in which the circle physically blocked the lines of people. On top of field experience that a straight breakthrough pushed the flock into the alleys, umbrella processions and substitute stairs were added as the response.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0248"></a>

### Habitat

The circle thickened only in daytime hours when the inspection-opening lid was open, and when the lid closed, its altitude dropped to the lower chord of the bridge. Over the school passage it could not become a circle and passed only as a thin band, and above the evacuation stairs the radius of the circle shrank to the width of the railing.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0250"></a>

### Needs and Economy

For the fortress to hold, it needed the sea wind, an open lid, and a promise that flashlight angles would not pierce the circle. Thrusting a flashlight at the center of the circle made the beaks descend along the light and tear at the bags of the rear rows, and turning the lights off doubled the inspection time and cut the transmission minutes.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0252"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

The density of the circle held only while the sea wind blew from one direction, and when the wind reversed, the division of altitude collapsed within an hour. After the collapse, droppings were left on the handrails of the substitute stairs, leaving slipping accidents for the next evacuation unit.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0254"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The individuals inside the circle divided only altitude without a leader, and the controllers only turned the lines of people. The breeding pairs'' division of the radius dissolved inside the circle, and the young clung to the lowest altitude, overlapping with the height of the bags.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0256"></a>

### Escalating Threat

While the circle was thin, one could cross without umbrellas by brushing only its edge. Once the sea wind had filled it, crossing was blocked except in an umbrella procession, and if the substitute stairs were opened late, the rear rows were caught under the circle.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0258"></a>

### Combat and Response

Once the air column above the inspection opening had been filled by the sea wind, pedestrians could not cross that circle except in an umbrella procession. Connecting the substitute stairs to the railing outside the circle first and turning the flashlight angles only toward the lower chord kept the beaks from coming down along the light. If the substitute stairs were opened late, the beaks inside the circle came down along the flashlights and tore the bag straps of the rear rows.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0260"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

The bridge controller had to try the umbrella procession for only two crossings and take it down before the third row. Keeping to the limit reduced the flow of passage but caused no damage to bags, and the rear waiting of G02-SC3 stayed within one evacuation unit. If it was not taken down, the flow of passage was kept, but the rear waiting time of G02-SC3 went past one evacuation unit.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0262"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC3 recorded that this air column was the stage for choosing whether to run the circle with umbrellas, to negotiate a substitute roost and new bells, or to postpone the inspection itself until after the breeding season. The work basket of G02-SC1 overlapped with the bells at the edge of the circle, and the transmission halt of G02-SC2 closed the lid and briefly thinned the circle.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0264"></a>
<a id="g02e12"></a>

### G02E12 · Radio Crow Fledgling ''Handrail Test''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: growth-stage type
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC3","G02-SC1","G02-SC2"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Common species |
| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Trial Occupation |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The fledgling ''Handrail Test'' is a young bird that tests the feel of a school handrail the day after removal. It cannot follow a breeding pair yet, and its diving call is still short. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0267"></a>

### Role and Body

The day after a forced demolition, a young body with short wings sat on the school handrail and tested its feel. Its dive call was still short, closer to curiosity than threat, and when the testing went past a week, that handrail became the new center of the route to school and the neighboring classes detoured around the stairs.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0269"></a>

### Origin

On top of the rule that repeated safety signals and food rewards, rather than nesting success, decided where the birds returned was added the occurrence that young individuals tested the school routes after a demolition. The warmth of a handrail from which helmets and poles had vanished remained as the first reward, and that reward fixed the fledgling''s place.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0271"></a>

### Habitat

It appeared on the handrail only on the morning route to school and hid behind the playground fence when the class bell rang. The adult seats at the factory roofs and the rail crossing were still beyond its weight to hold for long, and in the sea-wind weeks it used the low cables of the bridge railings as a second test seat.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0273"></a>

### Needs and Economy

To detach the testing from the handrail, a decoy roost and bug lamps had to be on the playground tower first. Without the decoy, droppings piled up on the handrail and the health duty officer lost the morning assignments, and moving the decoy every day made the fledgling follow it across the middle of the route to school.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0275"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

Its short wings learned to glide within a fortnight, and if it was chased off the handrail before then, the test seat moved to the handrails of the evacuation stairs. When its feathers were injured it rested at the drain beside the school infirmary, and that smell turned the lower-grade lines behind the building the next day.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0277"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The fledgling could not follow the breeding pair and only tested the feel of the handrail. Its helper-role warning calls were also too short for the adults to read, and the school-commute guide counted numbers only to tell whether the testing had become fixed or was passing through.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0279"></a>

### Escalating Threat

On the first day it sat for no more than a few seconds. When droppings piled up on the same handrail by the third day the detours began, and without a decoy roost the route to school the following week was redrawn around that handrail.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0281"></a>

### Combat and Response

When the test of a young individual sitting on the school handrail began the day after a forced demolition, its dive call was still short. Tying bug lamps and rough poles to the playground tower from dawn tilted the test sitting toward it. If the decoy roost could not be placed on the playground tower first, the same handrail became the new center of the route to school the following week.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0283"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Until the first injury after the demolition, the school and Haeryun could leave the top of the handrail as a temporary roost. Leaving it let less of the bridge pressure of G02-SC3 leak toward the school, but the lower-grade lines chose to walk under the wall. When one child was scratched, that window closed and the detour stairs of the neighboring classes used ten more minutes every day.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0285"></a>

### Scenario Links

The young testing left behind by the wholesale nest demolition of G02-SC3 began at this handrail. The horns of G02-SC1 were not yet connected to the fledgling''s expectation of food, and when the insect-lamp relocation of G02-SC2 overlapped with the playground decoy, the test seat moved out of the school.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0287"></a>
<a id="g02e13"></a>

### G02E13 · Radio Crow Elder Vocal Type ''Startup Sound''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: aging-stage type
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC2","G02-SC1","G02-SC3"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Common species |
| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Alarm Delay |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The elder vocal type ''Startup Sound'' stiffens its throat only at the sound of a transformer starting and keeps its wings folded even at a real fire alarm. It cannot lead the flock, and its silence worsens misclassification. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0290"></a>

### Role and Body

The aged individual beside the old transformer, whose neck stiffened only at the start-up hum, stayed on the insulator with its wings folded even when a real fire bell rang. The price of reacting late to unfamiliar alarms the more one specialized in familiar call patterns gathered in this body, and the flock scattered only after short-circuit smoke had covered the roof.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0292"></a>

### Origin

It was a remnant of the days when the pattern of three horn blasts, two inspection bells, and the transformer start-up hum was learned as food and danger. The imitation in the communal roost carried the new call patterns over into the young individuals, but it could not carry them into this aged syrinx, and that lag became the false role of damage.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0294"></a>

### Habitat

By day it clung to the insulators inside the transformer fence, and even when the night insect lamps came on it sat by the start-up hum rather than the lamps. It hardly ever appeared on the bridge or the school passage, and came down to the factory-roof exhaust vents only in the outage weeks when the start-up hum stopped.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0296"></a>

### Needs and Economy

For this body not to slow the flock''s scattering, a three-day training in a new low tone and the alley custom of using crow calls as an intrusion alarm had to be adjusted at the same time. Training alone reduced control malfunctions but left the cheap watch of the alleys, and removing the custom alone reduced malfunctions but took away the ears that had warned of intruders first.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0298"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

The elasticity of the syrinx dropped by one note each season, and the lowered note grew further from the new alarm bells. Its feathers were kept up with sand baths, but its auditory learning did not recover, and when it dried an injured wing on the transformer''s heat, that heat became one more false safety signal.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0300"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The aged individual could not lead the flock, and its silence instead fed the misjudgment. Even when the young individuals took to the air at the alarm bell, as long as this body stayed seated, the H02 classification kept the outage warning, and human control easily took that as grounds for halting work.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0302"></a>

### Escalating Threat

If its wings stayed folded even once the alarm bell rang, the misclassification began. Without the three-day training, the scattering came only after the smoke became visible, and that delay made a gap in both the transmission halt and the detection of intrusions in the alleys at once.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0304"></a>

### Combat and Response

The aged individual attuned only to the transformer start-up hum stayed on the insulator with its wings folded even when a real fire bell rang. Sending a short low tone different from the start-up hum from outside the fence every dawn for three days made its wing-folding a beat later. If the new low tone could not be played for three days, the short-circuit smoke covered the roof first, and only then did the flock scatter.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0306"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Whether to remove from the control network the custom of the alley residents using crow calls as an intrusion alarm was borne by the night duty officer without a signature. Not removing it let the misclassification of G02-SC2 halt the factories and the railway together, and reversing it within 48 hours allowed one more malfunction. Removing it reduced the malfunctions, and the cheap watch that G02-SC2 had kept disappeared from the alleys.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0308"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC2 completed the false outage at the point where the machine classification that raised calls to outage warnings overlapped with the aged individual''s silence. The horn pattern of G02-SC1 was the call pattern this syrinx forgot last, and the new inspection bell of G02-SC3 did not reach this body without the three-day training.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0310"></a>
<a id="g02e14"></a>

### G02E14 · Radio Crow Coexistence Roost ''Flag Altitude''

- Source batch: M003
- Former role class: coexistence type
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC3","G02-SC1","G02-SC2"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Common species |
| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Altitude Division |
| Common species, variants and command scope | The coexistence roost ''Flag Altitude'' is a gesture that divides altitude from the umbrella line only while the flag is on the railing during sea-wind week. Forcing an inspection after the flag comes down destroys that division. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0313"></a>

### Role and Body

Only while the sea-wind-week flag was up on the railing did the gesture of the designated roost divide altitude with the umbrella lines. Forced inspections after the flag came down broke that division, stacking transmission failures and walking injuries in the same afternoon, and pushing out the nests all at once made children the substitute targets of attack.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0315"></a>

### Origin

The negotiating device of a temporary flag recognizing a shared roost during the XT02 sea-wind weeks meshed with the choice of redividing the signals instead of complete removal, and became the gesture of coexistence. A local agreement for humans and birds to use the same infrastructure without wrecking each other''s alarm systems was bound to this flag.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0317"></a>

### Habitat

Only the railing where the flag hung and the twenty meters of altitude above it were the space of division, and this gesture did not appear on factory roofs without a flag. The pairs'' radii in the breeding season and the fledglings'' handrail tests after demolition avoided this space, and it overlapped with the umbrella lines only when the air column over the inspection opening was thick.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0319"></a>

### Needs and Economy

For the division to hold, the repair time slots, the retained stretches, and the relocation poles had to be bound in one ledger. When one item went empty it could be filled again only after the season had passed, and during that gap, either the transmission minutes took on a season''s risk of failure or children''s passage crossed the circle without umbrellas.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0321"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

When the flag cloth was torn by the sea wind and not replaced within the day, the division of altitude collapsed in the morning. The labor of moving nests one arm at a time with the relocation poles was allowed only after the chicks had fledged, and moving them before then made the fledglings leak out onto the school handrails.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0323"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The coexisting individuals had no chief and responded to the flag and the ledger items. Even the core''s mixed bells, under the flag, were answered only as far as the gaps in the umbrella lines, and the controllers looked at the empty items in the ledger to set the hour for lowering the flag.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0325"></a>

### Escalating Threat

In the mornings when the flag was up, altitude was divided and there were no injuries. Taking down only the flag in the afternoon and pushing the inspection through broke the division, and pushing out the nests all at once to open the bridge immediately left a season''s transmission gap and dives on the children''s side.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0327"></a>

### Combat and Response

While the sea-wind-week flag was up on the railing, the individuals of the designated roost divided altitude with the umbrella lines. Binding the repair time slots in the same ledger as the flag and opening the inspection opening only in the morning kept beaks and lamp angles from overlapping on one line. Forcing the inspection even after the flag came down broke the coexistence altitude, and transmission failures and walking injuries overlapped in the same afternoon.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0329"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

The single ledger that Haeryun mediated bound the repair time slots, the retained stretches, and the relocation poles, and whichever item went empty was filled again only after the season had passed. Choosing the immediate availability of G02-SC3 emptied all three items of the ledger at once, and children''s passage and transmission minutes paid the price in the same season. The price of pushing out the nests all at once to open the bridge immediately came back as a season''s transmission gap and substitute attacks on the children''s side.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0331"></a>

### Scenario Links

G02-SC3 recorded that unless the three branches of umbrella processions, substitute roosts, and post-breeding inspections were bound by this flag ledger, coexistence was put off by a season. The retirement of the horns in G02-SC1 made the repair time slots of the ledger be rewritten, and the insect-lamp relocation of G02-SC2 traded the retained stretches for the Nanji base.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0333"></a>
<a id="g02e15"></a>

### G02E15 · Signal Crow Bridge Convergence ''Synchronized Circle''

- Source batch: M004
- Former role class: scenario boss
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC1","G02-SC2","G02-SC3"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Swarm phenomenon |
| Battlefield unit | Event or phenomenon |
| Battlefield role | Synchronized dive |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Bridge Convergence ''Synchronized Circle'' is a scenario phase in which the perches of several families join into one circle. No commander has appeared; it is a convergence of sound patterns on an evening when sea winds and breeding overlap. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0336"></a>

### Role and Body

It was the scenario boss, a merged body that bound the roosts of several families into a single circle above the Han River bridge cables and the transmission inspection openings. Its flight feathers imitated three horn blasts and two inspection bells at once, and its eyes told apart helmets, poles, flashlight glints, and umbrella angles. It linked only sparks and transformer noise as clues to insects and workers and could not read the content of the radio waves, nor did it know whether a short circuit lay beyond the insulators.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0338"></a>

### Origin

In the season when the sea wind of the XT02 West Sea Granary Theater and the breeding season overlapped, call patterns learned separately at the Sangam towers and the Yongsan overhead wires mixed above the bridge, and this circle came into being. The communal roost of G02-SC3 was the original form of its body, and the learning by which young individuals made test roosts in the school passages after forced demolitions widened its radius.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0340"></a>

### Habitat

Its core place was the sky between the overhead-wire crossings of the Wangsimni Union''s Junction and the inspection openings of the Han River bridges. By day it scattered into the waste heat of the Gauge factory roofs on the Yeongdeungpo Line and gathered into the circle only in the evening when the sea wind strengthened, and when the wire repair baskets came up, part of it followed their track.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0342"></a>

### Needs and Economy

Its food was night insects, leftover protein from the markets, and small carcasses, and stripped fine wire and scraps of fiber went into its nests. When the HC02 Sinjeong Wheel House replaced the sheathing often and the material dwindled, it tore at railing signs and umbrella handles and territorial attacks grew, and when the sheathing was neglected, droppings covered the insulators and cut the available transmission time.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0344"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

In the less-cold weeks the breeding pairs divided one arc of the circle each to guard, and outside the breeding season several families joined. When its feathers were damaged by the sea wind, the imitation split and false alarms grew, and in the year after a demolition the circle gathered faster at the sound of the same inspection bell alone.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0346"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The breeding pairs only divided nest defense and food hauling; the merged circle had no leader. The mass flight was a temporary center that shared only call patterns, and even when H02 Seorin recorded the order of calls and the direction of flight, the halting of work was decided by the HC02 human controller. When one breeding individual vanished, a neighboring pair tried out the empty perch and clashes grew.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0348"></a>

### Escalating Threat

In the first stage, scouting individuals mimicked horns and inspection bells on the towers and railings and brought the call patterns to the circle. In the second stage, food signals and safety alarms mixed together, and dives on workers, school-route detours, and transmission halts followed in a chain. In the third stage, false alarms piled up three times at the same inspection opening, and the flock moved all at once at a single signal.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0350"></a>

### Combat and Response

Nets, gunshots, and aerial pursuit pushed the flock into the residential alleys and made every alarm call pattern distrusted. Turning the routes with visual screening cloths, umbrella processions, and substitute stairs, and moving the insect lures and inspection bells to other towers, split the learned signals. Success was measured not in carcasses but in fewer short circuits, zero walking injuries, available transmission time, and the false-alarm rate.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0352"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Pushing out the nests all at once to open the bridge immediately took away insect predation and the residents'' alarms, and children on foot and evacuees became substitute targets of the dives. If HC02 bound the repair time slots, the school and evacuation detours, the retained nest stretches, the relocation poles, and the temporary roosts of the sea-wind weeks in a single ledger, the same infrastructure could be shared at the cost of putting up with the confusion of relearning. Postponing the transmission inspection until after the breeding season reduced the clashes, but people bore a season''s risk of failure.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0354"></a>

### Scenario Links

The new signal above the bridge in G02-SC3 was the stage of this merged circle, and the three horns of G02-SC1 and the dive calls of G02-SC2 overlapped here into a single call pattern.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0356"></a>
<a id="g02e16"></a>

### G02E16 · Signal Crow Sea-Wind Variant ''Shore-Feather Perch''

- Source batch: M004
- Former role class: regional variant
- Links: {"houses":["HC02"],"scenarios":["G02-SC1","G02-SC2","G02-SC3"],"states":["free-city","government"],"synthetics":["H02"],"theaters":["XT02"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Common species |
| Battlefield unit | Pack or unit |
| Battlefield role | Coastal dive |
| Common species, variants and command scope | Sea-Wind Variant ''Shore-Feather Perch'' is a waterside branch that remembers the breakwater bell and lighthouse first. It does not imitate inland horn sequences, and its space does not overlap with the bridge circle even on the same evening. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0359"></a>

### Role and Body

It was a regional variant, a sea-wind type that wove scraps of fiber and net into the disused towers on the western waterside and the breakwater railings. The edges of its beak were worn by salt, so it chose wet fiber over fine wire, and its eyes read the bands of insects carried on the sea wind and the glint of the breakwater lighthouse. It remembered the waves and the breakwater bell before the inland horn pattern, and knew nothing of the content of the radio waves or the insulation of the distant bridges.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0361"></a>

### Origin

It split off as part of the nonbreeding flock moved temporarily to the western waterside following the sea wind and insect density pushing in from the XT02 side. After young individuals whose city towers had been demolished took a disused tower on the Nanji shore as a test roost, the breakwater bell became a food reward and changed where they returned the next year.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0363"></a>

### Habitat

Only in the dry and sea-wind weeks did it leave the Yeongdeungpo Line factory roofs and stay at the disused towers and breakwater railings of the western waterside. In the breeding season it did not return to the bridge circle but kept its shore roost, so even on the same evening as the inland merged circle, their spaces did not overlap.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0365"></a>

### Needs and Economy

Its food was insects driven in by the sea wind and leftovers from the waterside markets, and its nest material was fragments of net and wet fiber. Cutting off the inland sheathing did not reduce this variant, and moving the insect lamps to a disused tower made that tower a new feeding ground and base.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0367"></a>

### Life Cycle and Maintenance

When the sea wind died down it emptied the shore roost and went back to the factory roofs, but when the breakwater bell repeated for a season it put off its return. If its salty feathers were not rinsed in fresh water the imitation split, and after a forced demolition it tried out new seats on the awnings of the neighboring fish market.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0369"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

The shore roost had no leader either and shared only call patterns. It did not follow the horn imitation of the inland breeding pairs, and even when H02 raised a dive call as an outage warning, if the marker on site was the breakwater lighthouse, people sorted it out again. HC02 control decided only on halting waterside work and did not count the bridge circle together with this variant.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0371"></a>

### Escalating Threat

In the first stage it memorized the breakwater bell and the lighthouse blinking as food signals. In the second stage it gathered at a disused tower following the insect lamps and covered the waterside inspection basket. In the third stage, without a sea-wind-week flag, its test roosts spread to the fish-market awnings, and it made coastal walkers without school umbrellas the targets of its dives.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0373"></a>

### Combat and Response

Inland umbrella processions folded in the coastal wind and had no effect, and drone pursuit pushed the variant into the fish-market alleys. Switching off the insect lamps and changing the breakwater bell to a beat different from the inspection bell shook its learning for a few days, and screening only the basket with a visual cloth without nets was the safer course. The measures of success were waterside short circuits, coastal walking injuries, and the false-alarm rate.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0375"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Leaving the insect lamps on the disused shore tower to protect the inland school routes made the waterside labor and the fish market bear the cost of a new base. Moving the lighthouse back to the city towers made the school railings test roosts again. The choice of hanging the temporary sea-wind-week flag only on the breakwater and moving only the net nests with relocation poles delayed the restoration of transmission, but did not sell children''s passage and coastal livelihoods on the same evening.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0377"></a>

### Scenario Links

The insect-lamp relocation of G02-SC2 made this variant''s base, the sea-wind weeks of G02-SC3 grew its shore roost, and the retirement of the horns in G02-SC1 left the breakwater bell as its new feeding clock.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0379"></a>

## Habitat record

At dusk, black flocks settled tier upon tier on the factory pylons of the Daerim–Garibong Korean-Chinese corridor, the cables of the Han River bridges, and the industrial-park trolley wires of the Yeongdeungpo Line Gauge.

When the waste heat of the Yeongdeungpo Line Gauge factory roofs and the sparks of the overhead wires drew in night insects, the breeding pairs began to use these structures as both nest and feeding ground, and people called them the Large-billed Crow.

That large-billed crows guarded territories in the breeding season and adapted to human behavior rested on real ecology.

But the rule by which they learned the sequences of horns, inspection bells, and transformer start-up sounds as signals of food and danger and spread them through the communal roosts belonged to the history of this place.

They did not read the content of the radio waves.

They only remembered the relationships repeated among sparks, sounds, and the appearance of workers.

The core territory was the factory roofs and pylon clusters of the Yeongdeungpo Line and the rail overhead-wire junctions of the Wangsimni Union.

In the less-cold breeding weeks they circled in rings centered on their nests above the bridges, school passages, and evacuation stairs, and dived when a helmet or a long pole came within the radius.

In the non-breeding season several families joined the communal roosts, and moved briefly to the western waterfront following the sea wind from the XT02 West Sea Granary Theater and the density of insects.

When wire repairs began, some birds followed the work baskets, not because they were drawn to the equipment itself but because they chased the exposed insects, scraps of insulation, and the repeated sounds of work.

Because of this, the separate human routes of school commutes, evacuation, and transmission inspection could all be blocked at once beneath a single pylon.

They fed on insects, protein left at the markets, and small carcasses, and stripped fine wire and scraps of fiber went into their nests.

When HC02 Sinjeong Wheel House replaced wire insulation often, the risk of short circuits and the supply of nest material both fell, but the breeding pairs that lost their material could tear at the railings and signs of the nearby school routes and step up their territorial attacks.

Conversely, when damaged insulation was left alone, the nest supply stabilized but the chance of short circuits and fire grew.

When droppings built up on the insulators, transmission uptime fell, and when maintenance crews came in the dives increased, so a cycle formed in which work stoppages let the fouling pile up again.

Because the benefit to one zone that drove the crows out moved on as danger to other bridges and residential alleys, simple removal alone could not erase the cost.

In the less-cold weeks the breeding pairs divided a single pylon between them to guard.

While one bird defended the nest and chicks, the other carried food, and young helpers joined in the alarm calls and dives.

The non-breeding communal roosts had no leader and shared only one another''s sound patterns and reactions.

When one breeding bird vanished, neighboring pairs tested the empty roost and territorial clashes rose, and after forced removal the young made new places on nearby school passages or evacuation stairs.

What decided where they returned the next year was not only nest success but the safety signals and food rewards repeated there.

A familiar sequence such as three horn blasts or two inspection bells could spread through a whole communal roost within days, but this was social learning, not a bodily change passed through generations.

A flock grown too used to a particular sound pattern paid the price of reacting late to an unfamiliar real alarm.

They read the outline of a helmet, the angle of a pole, and the glint of a flashlight by sight, and by hearing they separated the order in which high-voltage noise, horns, and an infant''s cry followed one another.

They did not know the technical meaning of a signal; they linked it to the experience that insects, food, workers, or a threat to the nest had appeared after a particular sound.

H02 Seorin recorded the source and repetition count of a sound pattern, the nest radius, the flight direction, and the order of calls separately.

But whether a dive call was the forerunner of a real short circuit or a feeding response to an insect lamp could be judged only when people checked the markings on site against the state of the insulation.

When automatic classification combined with the residents'' old alarm customs, wrong judgments reinforced one another, and a single small false alarm could spread into a transmission shutdown and the closure of evacuation routes.

The interference of the Large-billed Crow first appeared as the collecting of sound patterns.

Scout birds mimicked horns and inspection bells from the pylons and railings, and carried the sounds tied to food back to the communal roost.

In the next stage, food signals and safety alarms became tangled, and dives at workers, school-route detours, and transmission stops followed one after another.

When false alarms piled up three times at the same inspection hatch, the maintenance hours, the inspection bells, and the residents'' alarm customs had to be reset together instead of tearing out the nests.

Bending the route with sight-blocking cloth, umbrella columns, and alternative stairs was safer than pushing straight through.

Moving the insect-lure lamps and inspection bells to a different pylon could also separate the learned signals.

Gunfire and aerial pursuit drove the flock into residential alleys and left distrust of every alarm sound.

Results were measured not by the number of birds removed but by fewer short circuits, pedestrian injuries prevented, transmission uptime, and the false-alarm rate.

G02-SC1 ‘Three Horn Blasts’ began when a new horn sequence introduced by a Yeongdeungpo Line work crew overlapped with the feeding signal.

The crows that learned it at the communal roost swarmed the work baskets instead of withdrawing from the warning.

Scrapping the horn sequence reduced the birds'' approach, but work slowed while a safety procedure familiar to people was built again.

Teaching a separate feeding signal could separate the two sound patterns in the long run, but meant accepting more false alarms for several days.

Moving the feeding to horn-free nights protected the daytime school routes, but passed the danger and fatigue on to the night workers.

HC02 and H02 could not judge which signal was more legitimate; they could only choose whether to change human habit or the birds'' survival learning first.

In G02-SC2 ‘The False Blackout Alarm,’ H02 classified repeated dive calls as the forerunner of a blackout, but the real cause turned out to be a night insect lamp.

Because residents had long used the crows'' calls as a cheap disaster alarm, machine classification and human custom confirmed one another and set off a chain of transmission stops on the Yeongdeungpo Line and in the Wangsimni Union.

Keeping transmission running until visual confirmation reduced needless stoppages but left the risk of missing a real short circuit.

Moving the insect lamp to a disused pylon untangled the present confusion, but that pylon then had to be recognized as a new feeding ground and ecological base.

Dropping the crow alarm from the control network entirely reduced malfunctions, but the residents'' watch network that had first noticed intruders and equipment faults disappeared with it.

The cost of keeping inaccurate alarms and the cost of losing cheap surveillance fell on different groups.

G02-SC3 ‘The New Signal over the Bridge’ reached its final stage when the XT02 sea wind and the breeding season coincided and the air above the bridges and transmission inspection hatches became a communal roost for many families.

Because sound patterns learned in different places had mixed, the whole flock moved at once at a single inspection bell.

Running the evacuation route as umbrella columns reduced direct attacks, but the flow of traffic fell and the refugees at the back waited long.

Providing alternative roosts together with a new inspection bell laid the base for long-term coexistence, but meant accepting the confusion and management cost of the relearning period.

Putting off transmission inspection until after the breeding season reduced clashes but meant carrying the risk of breakdown for a whole season.

The agreement HC02 brokered bound into one the maintenance hours, school and evacuation detours, the zones where nests were left in place, the use of relocation poles, and the recognition of temporary roosts in the weeks of the XT02 sea wind.

Pushing the nests out all at once opened the bridges briefly, but insect predation and the residents'' alarms vanished together, and children and refugees became the new targets of attack.

The choice to divide the signals again rather than remove the birds entirely meant giving up immediate convenience, but it was the only bargain that let humans and birds share the same infrastructure without wrecking each other''s alarm systems.

To protect the Large-billed Crow and the infrastructure together, the Yeongdeungpo Line and the Wangsimni Union had to keep a history of sounds for each pylon.

HC02 had to renew the rules of use before and after the breeding season.

Horns and inspection bells were not changed at will, the time of occurrence, food exposure, and dive direction were recorded together, and the insulation state and nest radius confirmed by the workers on site were always added to H02''s judgments.

Management results were read separately as transmission uptime, dives per thousand work hours, the share of alarms cancelled after visual confirmation, the amount of insulator fouling, the number of fledged young per nest, and the reuse rate of alternative roosts.

When a short circuit or large-scale nest removal caused a failure, the old sound patterns were halted for three days and the work zone closed with visual markers alone, and then the insect lamps and food exposure resumed first at the alternative roosts.

After that, the new inspection bell was not spread all at once but played pylon by pylon at staggered times, and main-line work was restored once the mimicry at the communal roost had settled.

Recovery was judged not by whether the crows had gone but by whether they spread out to the designated roosts instead of swarming at alarm sounds, and whether residents could tell a real breakdown from a bird reaction.

When fixed silent hours and maintenance timetables held for a long time, market haulers and unauthorized scavengers too came to watch the crows'' movements and guess the moments when the equipment stood empty.

So to stop the side effect of a predictable signal system lowering bird clashes while becoming a clock for human intrusion, the published safety signals had to be kept while the actual inspection order and material routes were run irregularly.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0445"></a>

## Linked scenarios

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0446"></a>

### G02-SC1 · Three Horns

- Stage: 1
- Trigger: The new horn order of the Yeongdeungpo Line work crew overlaps the food-drop signal, and the crows gather on the work basket.
- Actors: free-city, HC02, H02
- Ecological mechanism: A sound shape learned at the communal roost triggers the expectation of food before it triggers a warning.
- Choices: Abolish the horn order / Train a separate food signal / Move the food drop to a hornless night
- Outcomes: Abolishing it slows the work-safety procedure, and retraining accepts several days of false alarms. The night move protects the daytime school route but shifts the burden onto the night workers.
- Moral cost: One of the two has to change: the safety signal familiar to people, or the survival signal the birds have learned.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0448"></a>

### G02-SC2 · The False Blackout Alarm

- Stage: 2
- Trigger: H02 classified the repeated dive calls as a precursor of a blackout, but the real cause is the night insect lamp.
- Actors: free-city, government, HC02, H02
- Ecological mechanism: The birds'' alarm custom and the machine''s sound classification reinforce each other and chain-trigger a transmission stop.
- Choices: Keep transmitting until a visual check / Move the insect lamp to an abandoned pylon / Drop the crow alarm from the control net
- Outcomes: Keeping the transmission accepts the risk of a real short, and relocating makes the substitute pylon an ecological strongpoint. Dropping the alarm loses the residents'' intrusion-detection net.
- Moral cost: Discarding an inaccurate ecological alarm reduces malfunctions, but the cheap disaster watch disappears too.
- Source dossier: G02

<a id="Hostile-Group-G02-heading-0450"></a>

### G02-SC3 · A New Signal on the Bridge

- Stage: 3
- Trigger: The XT02 sea wind overlaps the breeding season, and the air above the bridge and the transmission inspection hatch becomes a communal roost.
- Actors: free-city, government, XT02, HC02
- Ecological mechanism: Several family groups share sound shapes learned in different places and move all at once to one signal.
- Choices: Run the evacuation route as an umbrella column / Negotiate an alternate roost and a new inspection bell / Postpone the transmission inspection until after the breeding season
- Outcomes: The umbrella column reduces throughput, and a new signal needs a relearning period. Postponing the inspection avoids the ecological collision but carries the risk of a transmission failure for one season.
- Moral cost: The bridge''s immediate availability and the long-term coexistence of transmission and the birds'' alarm cannot be had at the same time.
- Source dossier: G02
