| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Oil-leak accumulation |
| Common species, variants and command scope | An old lifting body dragging a sagging arm and hydraulic-oil trails. It followed behind the lifting swarms, unable to set down the material it raised and piling it in passages; as leakage worsened it lowered the load ceiling, and given a seal it recovered for one shift. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0096"></a>

### Role and Body

An aging-stage form: an old lifting unit that moves with one sagging arm, leaving a trail of hydraulic fluid.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0098"></a>

### Origin

Repeated work wore down its cylinder, but it never received a disposal order and still seeks its last load.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0100"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

It prefers flat stockyards and needs hydraulic seals, absorbent cloth, and low-power charging.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0102"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

As leakage worsens, it lowers its load limit; a new seal restores it for one shift.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0104"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It follows the lifting group, unable to put down the materials it raises, and piles them in the passage.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0106"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 brings an oil leak, Stage 2 a pileup of materials, and Stage 3 blocks passage with its drooping arm and load.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0108"></a>

### Engagement and Response

Support the load and release the pressure; cutting off the arm would make the materials fall.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0110"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

It can carry light loads, but someone must decide whether to give it a precious seal before the residents'' pump.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0112"></a>

### Scenario Links

G16-SC1, G16-SC2.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0114"></a>
<a id="g16e14"></a>

### G16E14 · Chalk-Line Keeper

- Source batch: M026
- Former role class: coexistence type
- Links: {"houses":["HP02"],"scenarios":["G16-SC1","G16-SC2","G16-SC3"],"states":["arc"],"synthetics":["H16"],"theaters":["XT01"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Chalk-line defense |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A small survey cart with a broad optical panel reading people''s chalk lines and stop flags. Prioritizing a nearer chalk line over the warden''s drawings, it blocked the way before the welding bodies; when rain erased lines it waited for human confirmation, and given new lines it renewed the grid exception. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0117"></a>

### Role and Body

A coexistence form: a small survey cart with a broad optical panel that reads human chalk lines and stop flags.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0119"></a>

### Origin

A safety mode that gave priority to a site supervisor''s manual corrections survived as an independent protocol.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0121"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

It travels between residents'' temporary construction zones and tool walls, needing bright chalk, lens cloth, and a low charge.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0123"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

If rain erases a line, it waits for human confirmation; a new line updates its grid exceptions.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0125"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It gives a nearby chalk line priority over the chief unit''s drawing and blocks welding units.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0127"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 creates only a boundary dispute; Stage 2 delays reinforcement and thus rescue; Stage 3 paralyzes the entire zone through a dispute over shelter space.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0129"></a>

### Engagement and Response

Resetting the boundary with chalk and a stop flag halts its survey; a strong flash impairs its reading.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0131"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

It helps protect homes, but the community must distribute the authority to draw chalk lines and clearly rank competing boundaries.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0133"></a>

### Scenario Links

G16-SC1, G16-SC2, G16-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0135"></a>
<a id="g16e15"></a>

### G16E15 · Hundred-Floor Unfinished Tower

- Source batch: M026
- Former role class: scenario boss
- Links: {"houses":["HP02"],"scenarios":["G16-SC2","G16-SC3"],"states":["arc"],"synthetics":["H16"],"theaters":["XT01"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Habitat site or facility |
| Battlefield unit | Fixed site |
| Battlefield role | Unfinished-tower construction |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A giant construction body with crane arms, survey beams, and a distribution wall joined to a vertical frame. A scenario boss, but no biological king of the corps — a stronghold where the layers of an uncompleted high-rise drawing reactivated at once; when resources cut it abandoned floors one by one, and when exception layers passed half it came down to a negotiable state. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0138"></a>

### Role and Body

A scenario boss: an enormous construction unit whose crane arm, surveying beam, and distribution wall join a vertical frame.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0140"></a>

### Origin

Every layer of an unfinished high-rise drawing restarted at once and assembled into a tower.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0142"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

Fixed to a large temporary construction zone in Suseo, it needs immense power, wire, anchors, and cooling water.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0144"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

When resources run out, it relinquishes floors one by one; if exception layers become a majority, it enters a negotiable state.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0146"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It assigns work by floor to the entire G16 group and treats homes as unrecorded space.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0148"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 is surveying, Stage 2 large-scale demolition and lifting, and Stage 3 a total grid closure.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0150"></a>

### Engagement and Response

Cut power floor by floor and freeze the drawing hash; blasting the foundation would collapse both the common utility duct and the homes.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0152"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

It could be completed as a barrier and reinforcement tower, but the cost of materials and relocating residents must be agreed upon.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G16-heading-0154"></a>

### Scenario Links

G16-SC2, G16-SC3.

